2025-05-01 03:30:57 - Thread-1478 (execute) - INFO - [Exploration]
Relevant columns and their potential usage 
• SalaryDataset."CompanyName":            company identifier to group salaries  
• SalaryDataset."Location":               city name used to filter on Mumbai, Pune, New Delhi, Hyderabad  
• SalaryDataset."Salary":                 text field that must be cleaned and converted to a number in order to take averages  
• SalaryDataset."index":                  row identifier (not needed for the analysis but can be used to sample data)  

Typical conditions that will be used  
• WHERE "Location" IN ('Mumbai','Pune','New Delhi','Hyderabad')       –– limit rows to the four requested cities  
• REPLACE(REPLACE(REPLACE("Salary",'₹',''),',',''),'/yr','')          –– strip currency sign, commas, and “/yr” text  
• CAST( … AS REAL)                                                      –– turn the cleaned string into a numeric value  
• WINDOW‑FUNCTION  RANK() OVER (PARTITION BY "Location" ORDER BY avg_city_salary DESC) –– pick the 5 highest‑paid companies inside every city  

Below are ten exploratory → progressively more complex SQLite queries (each limited to 20 rows where applicable) that build all the necessary steps and end with the final answer query.

```sql
-- 1. Description: look at the distinct locations present in the salary table
SELECT DISTINCT "Location"
FROM   "SalaryDataset"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. Description: sample the raw salary strings we will have to clean
SELECT "Location",
       "CompanyName",
       "Salary"
FROM   "SalaryDataset"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3. Description: test‑drive a cleaning step that removes the currency symbol,
--    commas, and the trailing “/yr”, then casts to a number
SELECT "Salary",
       CAST(REPLACE(REPLACE(REPLACE("Salary",'₹',''),',',''),'/yr','') AS REAL) 
           AS salary_clean
FROM   "SalaryDataset"
WHERE  "Salary" IS NOT NULL
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4. Description: average salary for every company in the whole country
SELECT   "CompanyName",
         AVG(
               CAST(REPLACE(REPLACE(REPLACE("Salary",'₹',''),',',''),'/yr','') AS REAL)
             ) AS avg_salary_country
FROM     "SalaryDataset"
GROUP BY "CompanyName"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5. Description: average salary for every company inside each city
SELECT   "Location",
         "CompanyName",
         AVG(
               CAST(REPLACE(REPLACE(REPLACE("Salary",'₹',''),',',''),'/yr','') AS REAL)
             ) AS avg_salary_city
FROM     "SalaryDataset"
GROUP BY "Location","CompanyName"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6. Description: restrict previous query to the four target cities
SELECT   "Location",
         "CompanyName",
         AVG(
               CAST(REPLACE(REPLACE(REPLACE("Salary",'₹',''),',',''),'/yr','') AS REAL)
             ) AS avg_salary_city
FROM     "SalaryDataset"
WHERE    "Location" IN ('Mumbai','Pune','New Delhi','Hyderabad')
GROUP BY "Location","CompanyName"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7. Description: rank companies by their city‑level average salary
--    and keep their rank side by side
SELECT   "Location",
         "CompanyName",
         AVG(
               CAST(REPLACE(REPLACE(REPLACE("Salary",'₹',''),',',''),'/yr','') AS REAL)
             ) AS avg_salary_city,
         RANK() OVER (PARTITION BY "Location"
                      ORDER BY AVG(
                               CAST(REPLACE(REPLACE(REPLACE("Salary",'₹',''),',',''),'/yr','') AS REAL)
                             ) DESC) AS city_rank
FROM     "SalaryDataset"
WHERE    "Location" IN ('Mumbai','Pune','New Delhi','Hyderabad')
GROUP BY "Location","CompanyName"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8. Description: using the rank just created, keep only the top‑5 companies in each city
SELECT *
FROM (
        SELECT  "Location",
                "CompanyName",
                AVG(
                      CAST(REPLACE(REPLACE(REPLACE("Salary",'₹',''),',',''),'/yr','') AS REAL)
                    ) AS avg_salary_city,
                RANK() OVER (PARTITION BY "Location"
                             ORDER BY AVG(
                                 CAST(REPLACE(REPLACE(REPLACE("Salary",'₹',''),',',''),'/yr','') AS REAL)
                             ) DESC) AS city_rank
        FROM    "SalaryDataset"
        WHERE   "Location" IN ('Mumbai','Pune','New Delhi','Hyderabad')
        GROUP BY "Location","CompanyName"
     )
WHERE  city_rank <= 5
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9. Description: calculate the single overall national average salary
SELECT AVG(
           CAST(REPLACE(REPLACE(REPLACE("Salary",'₹',''),',',''),'/yr','') AS REAL)
         ) AS national_avg_salary
FROM   "SalaryDataset";
```

```sql
-- 10. Description: FINAL ANSWER – top‑5 companies by average salary in each
--     of the four specified cities, plus the overall national average in a
--     four‑column output (Location, Company Name, Average Salary in State,
--     Average Salary in Country)
WITH national AS (
    SELECT AVG(
             CAST(REPLACE(REPLACE(REPLACE("Salary",'₹',''),',',''),'/yr','') AS REAL)
           ) AS national_avg_salary
    FROM   "SalaryDataset"
),
city_avgs AS (
    SELECT  "Location",
            "CompanyName",
            AVG(
                  CAST(REPLACE(REPLACE(REPLACE("Salary",'₹',''),',',''),'/yr','') AS REAL)
                ) AS avg_salary_city,
            RANK() OVER (PARTITION BY "Location"
                         ORDER BY AVG(
                             CAST(REPLACE(REPLACE(REPLACE("Salary",'₹',''),',',''),'/yr','') AS REAL)
                         ) DESC) AS city_rank
    FROM    "SalaryDataset"
    WHERE   "Location" IN ('Mumbai','Pune','New Delhi','Hyderabad')
    GROUP BY "Location","CompanyName"
)
SELECT  c."Location"                        AS "Location",
        c."CompanyName"                     AS "Company Name",
        ROUND(c.avg_salary_city, 2)         AS "Average Salary in State",
        ROUND(n.national_avg_salary, 2)     AS "Average Salary in Country"
FROM    city_avgs  AS c
CROSS JOIN national AS n
WHERE   c.city_rank <= 5
ORDER BY c."Location",
         c.avg_salary_city DESC,
         c."CompanyName";
```

Each block incrementally explores the data and logic, culminating in a ready‑to‑run query that returns the requested four‑column report of the best‑paying five companies in Mumbai, Pune, New Delhi, and Hyderabad together with the national average salary for comparison.
